  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><title>Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table</title>
<para id="para-00001">Identify the \(x\)-intercepts and vertex of the quadratic from the table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
  <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
        <para id="para-00002">-5</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00003">-3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00004">-1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00005">0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00006">1</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
        <para id="para-00007">16</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00008">0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00009">-2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00010">0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para id="para-00011">16</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody></tgroup></table>

<para id="para-00012">The \(x\)-intercepts are where the \(y\)-values are 0.</para>
<para id="para-00013">This occurs at \((-3,0)\) and \((0,0)\).</para>
<para id="para-00014">The vertex occurs where the function will create a mirror image, or be symmetrical for all points. This often occurs halfway between the two zeros (when there are two zeros).</para>
<para id="para-00015">For this function, the vertex is at \((-1,-2)\).</para>
<title> Try It: Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table </title>
<example id="exam-00001"><exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00001">
    
    <para id="para-00016">Determine the vertex from the table:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
            <para id="para-00017">5</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00018">6</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00019">7</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00020">8</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00021">9</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00022">10</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(f(x)\)</emphasis></entry><entry>
            <para id="para-00023">0</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00024">4</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00025">8</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00026">4</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00027">0</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para id="para-00028">-2</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00029">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution id="sol-00001">
  <para id="para-00030">Here is how to determine the vertex from the table:</para>
  
  <para id="para-00031">The zeros are located at \((5, 0)\) and \((9, 0)\).
    The vertex will be halfway between the two zeros. This occurs at \(x = 7\).</para>
  
  <para id="para-00032">The point in the table at \(x = 7\) is \((7, 8)\).</para>
  
  <para id="para-00033">This is the vertex.</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
